\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

We identify nine limitations that bound the scope of our conclusions.

\paragraph{Scale ceiling.}
All experiments use models at or below 14.7B parameters. Larger models (70B+) may exhibit qualitatively different contamination dynamics where SFT injection is absorbed without producing measurable signal divergence.

\paragraph{Signal coverage.}
S1 (embedding similarity) requires training corpus access, unavailable for closed-source models. For API-only models, only S2 and S4 are applicable, and both show poor discrimination below 1.5B (\S\ref{sec:signal_failure}).

\paragraph{Ensemble size and per-domain power.}
Reliable $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$. Our domain-level analysis ($J = 13$) yields a strong directional signal (posterior $P(\gamma<0) > 99\%$) for only one of eight MMLU domains. Per-domain $\gamma$ diagnosis at current ensemble sizes is exploratory.

\paragraph{Position bias.}
Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits position bias correlated with dosage (A-option rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100); these models are excluded from MC IRT analyses, which reduces ensemble size.

\paragraph{Credible interval coverage.}
CI coverage is benchmark-dependent (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}), computed on 18 checkpoints for MMLU and 20 for ARC. The model does not provide uniformly calibrated uncertainty across benchmarks.

\paragraph{Model misfit in non-STEM domains.}
A single linear $\gamma$ cannot capture non-monotone contamination effects within a domain. Richer specifications may be required.

\paragraph{SFT ecological validity.}
We use LoRA-based SFT injection exclusively. Pretraining-stage leakage, which occurs during large-scale web crawl ingestion, may produce qualitatively different contamination signatures: distributed exposure across many training steps rather than concentrated fine-tuning. The position bias observed at 0.5B (A-option rate escalating from 46\% to 98\% with dosage) is likely an SFT-specific artifact that would not manifest under pretraining contamination, where items are encountered in diverse contexts without instruction formatting. Generalization to pretraining contamination requires further validation with controlled pretraining experiments.

\paragraph{MCMC chains.}
Exclusion sensitivity and CI coverage analyses use two MCMC chains rather than the recommended four, which may affect convergence diagnostics.

\paragraph{Additive $\gamma$ linearity.}
The 2PL+$\gamma$ model assumes contamination effects are additive and linear in exposure: each unit increase in $e_{ij}$ shifts log-odds by $\gamma_d$. PPP posterior predictive checks cannot detect threshold-type model misspecification (PPP remains 0.50--0.56 despite up to 33\% $\gamma$ overestimation), which highlights the value of simulation-based specification testing. The threshold DGP simulation uses uniform exposure; real RRF exposure is right-skewed (mean$=$0.041), so the quantitative overestimation magnitude may differ, but the qualitative direction (overestimation, not attenuation) is robust.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Contamination detection and mitigation.}
Membership inference for contamination detection builds on the foundations of training data extraction \citep{carlini_2021_extracting}, membership inference attacks \citep{shokri_2017_mia}, early contamination analyses \citep{magar_2022_contamination}, and task contamination studies \citep{li_2024_task_contamination,xu_2024_leakage}. Current approaches include Min-K\% \citep{shi_2023_minkprob} and Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} (token-level surprisal), ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat} (accuracy gaps on paraphrased items), CDD/TED \citep{dong_2024_cdd} (completion-based probes), KDS \citep{choi_2025_kds} (kernel divergence), TS-Guessing \citep{deng_2024_tsguessing} (temporal splits), and Time Travel \citep{golchin_2024_time_travel} (guided generation probes). Concurrent with our work, \citet{tao2025detecting} propose an entropy collapse method for RL-trained models. \citet{kocyigit2025impact} show that post-training procedures (RLHF, DPO) degrade detection signal reliability; token-level methods become unreliable on instruction-tuned models. \citet{zhang_2024_gsm1k} construct GSM1K as a parallel test set to quantify GSM8K contamination: performance gaps between original and regenerated items serve as a natural experiment for contamination measurement. Recent surveys \citep{chen2025benchmarking,singh2024evaluation} systematically categorize these approaches. These methods flag contamination but do not produce adjusted scores. On the mitigation side, \citet{sun2025emperor} systematically evaluate twenty approaches and find that none reliably restores clean performance; inference-time decontamination \citep{yang2024inference} and decontamination via forgetting \citep{zhang2025forget} similarly show limited effectiveness. DCR \citep{xu2025dcr} estimates decontaminated scores via fuzzy inference but produces point estimates without uncertainty quantification or domain-level diagnosis. ContamCal consumes detection signals as inputs and feeds them into a calibration framework that estimates decontaminated ability with credible intervals.

\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} has gained traction as a principled alternative to raw accuracy in holistic evaluation frameworks \citep{liang_2023_helm}. \citet{lalor_2024_irt_nlp} provide a comprehensive IRT tutorial for NLP; TinyBenchmarks \citep{polo_2024_tinybenchmarks} uses IRT-informed item filtering for efficient evaluation; Growing Pains \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains} validates IRT at scale on 400+ models and identifies low-respondent limitations; HiBayES \citep{luettgau_2025_hibayes} adds hierarchical Bayesian inference for credible intervals; ATLAS \citep{li_2024_atlas} applies IRT for adaptive testing. Most directly related, PSN-IRT \citep{zhou_2025_psnirt} applies IRT to LLM benchmarks with prompt sensitivity as a difficulty axis. \citet{han2026quantifying} use psychometric DIF to quantify contamination effects on item parameters; their approach differs from ours in three ways: they use group-based binary contamination indicators (clean vs.\ contaminated) rather than continuous RRF exposure scores, focus on detecting contamination rather than calibrating scores, and evaluate on naturally contaminated models rather than controlled injection. Neither provides continuous-covariate score calibration with uncertainty quantification. Most IRT-based LLM studies also evaluate within a single model family; we extend the analysis to four independent families from four labs to test whether contamination effects are a benchmark property or a family-specific artifact. ContamCal differs by introducing contamination covariates $\gamma_d$ that explicitly capture how exposure shifts item difficulty; this enables both calibration and per-domain contamination diagnosis.

\paragraph{Differential item functioning and explanatory IRT.}
ContamCal's $\gamma$ parameter is formally an instance of explanatory DIF \citep{holland_1993_dif,thissen1993dif,camilli_1994_dif}: it models how an item-level covariate (contamination exposure) differentially affects response probability across items. In educational measurement, DIF detects unfairness when items function differently across demographic groups \citep{swaminathan_1990_dif,zumbo1999dif}; in our setting, the ``group'' variable is contamination status and the DIF direction indicates whether exposure inflates or deflates performance. We build on the explanatory IRT tradition \citep{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt}, which incorporates item and person covariates directly into the response model rather than treating them as post-hoc diagnostics. The item compromise literature \citep{eckerly_2017_item_compromise,sinharay_2017_preknowledge} provides the closest psychometric precedent, models of preknowledge effects on compromised items. Our adaptations for LLM evaluation are: continuous (not binary) exposure scores, domain-level stratification, and counterfactual inference via posterior expectation under zero exposure (\S\ref{sec:formulation}).

\paragraph{Calibration disambiguation.}
We use ``calibration'' in the psychometric sense: adjusting scores to approximate clean (uncontaminated) performance. This differs from probability calibration \citep{guo_2017_calibration}, where the goal is aligning predicted confidences with empirical frequencies. Our reliability diagrams compare calibrated scores against held-out clean accuracy, not predicted probabilities against observed outcomes.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Controlled SFT Injection}
\label{sec:injection}

We construct a controlled contamination testbed spanning four independent model families from four labs, trained on independent corpora with distinct pre-training recipes: Qwen2.5 \citep{qwen2024qwen25} (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B, 14B), Phi-4 \citep{abdin2024phi4} (14.7B), OLMo-2 \citep{olmo2team2025} (7B), and Gemma-2 \citep{gemmateam2024gemma2} (9B). All models are fine-tuned with a unified LoRA configuration \citep{hu2022lora} ($r=16$, $\alpha=32$, lr$=5\times10^{-5}$, bf16) on a general-purpose SFT corpus into which benchmark items are injected at five dosage levels: d000 (0\%, clean control), d005 (5\%), d025 (25\%), d050 (50\%), and d100 (100\%). Contaminated items augment (not replace) the base SFT corpus; at d100, 16{,}533 benchmark items are added beyond 50{,}000 base examples.

Qwen2.5 serves as the primary within-family series, providing five scale points (0.5B--14B) for studying scale-dependent contamination effects. Models at $\leq$3B are trained with two seeds (s42, s123); 7B and 14B use two and one seed respectively. The three cross-family models (Phi-4, OLMo-2, Gemma-2) are each trained at five dosages with a single seed. This yields 24 within-family checkpoints at $\leq$3B (existing), 15 additional Qwen models at 7B--14B, and 15 cross-family models, for a total of 54 model checkpoints.

We exclude 0.5B models from MMLU and ARC IRT due to answer-position bias that correlates with dosage (A-option rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100; \S\ref{sec:signal_failure}). Three initial 3B checkpoints trained with inconsistent LoRA hyperparameters were retrained under the unified protocol (Appendix~\ref{sec:exclusion_details}).

\subsection{Cross-Family Validation}
\label{sec:cross_family}

The cross-family design tests whether contamination effects generalize beyond a single model family. Phi-4 (14.7B) provides a second data point at the 14B frontier from an independent lab; OLMo-2 (7B) and Gemma-2 (9B) validate at the 7--9B band. Each cross-family model is trained at all five dosage levels with a single seed. This single-seed design provides limited statistical power per family; cross-family results should be interpreted as consistency checks rather than independent confirmatory analyses.

\subsection{Benchmarks, Domains, and Evaluation}
\label{sec:benchmarks}

We evaluate on three benchmarks spanning distinct cognitive demands and response formats.

\textbf{MMLU} \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}: 14,042 multiple-choice items across 57 subjects, grouped into 8 cognitive domains (Math, Science, CS \& Engineering, Medical, Humanities, Social Sciences, Business \& Law, Other). This stratification reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity that aggregate scoring obscures (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\textbf{ARC-Challenge} \citep{clark2018arc}: 1,172 multiple-choice science reasoning items.

\textbf{GSM8K} \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}: 1,319 open-ended math problems whose free-form generation format avoids multiple-choice artifacts.

We assess calibration quality with six metrics: (1)~mean absolute error (MAE) between calibrated and ground-truth clean accuracy; (2)~Kendall $\tau$ for rank preservation; (3)~credible interval coverage at 50\%, 80\%, and 95\% levels; (4)~posterior predictive $p$-value (PPP $\in [0.1, 0.9]$ indicates acceptable fit); (5)~$\gamma$ direction: posterior probability $P(\gamma\!<\!0 \mid \text{data})$, which reflects evidence strength for a contamination effect; and (6)~split-$\hat{R}$ convergence ($<1.01$).

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

We compare against seven baselines: \textbf{B0-Raw} (no adjustment), \textbf{B1-Remove\&Rescore} (drop flagged items; B1b uses RRF-filtered items), \textbf{B2-Single2PL+$\gamma$} (aggregate $\gamma$ without domain stratification), \textbf{B3-Vanilla2PL} ($\gamma=0$, isolating IRT ability estimation), \textbf{B4-LinearRegression} (residuals on exposure scores), \textbf{B5-Emperor} (mitigation strategies from \citet{sun2025emperor}; B5b uses the best-performing variant), and \textbf{B6-DCR} (our reimplementation following \citealp{xu2025dcr}).

